WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.770
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:06.200
Of all the planets that I’ve lived on, I’d
have to say Earth is probably my favorite.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.389
And despite our best efforts to ruin it, it
still has some pretty spectacular features

00:00:11.389 --> 00:00:15.769
like the jungles of the Amazon, the incredible
mountain ranges that litter the Earth’s

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:20.179
crust, the Great Lakes of northern America
and that Chinese takeaway on West street,

00:00:20.179 --> 00:00:22.060
you know, the one with the free prawn crackers.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:27.699
The thing is, us humans are a picky bunch
and we’re never quite happy with the way

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:28.699
things are.

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:33.470
There are phones to upgrade, windows to repaint,
houses to extend and suspicious pills to buy

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.250
from your junk email folder.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.200
But what about the planet itself?

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.840
Can we fix any of those little issues that
have been bugging us for years like how the

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:48.370
Sahara is a bit too hot, Australia is definitely
too far away and can I just have a bit of

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.510
Greenland as I really don’t think anyone
it using it.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:57.770
So grab a paint brush and let’s see if its
possible to change the planet to suit our

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:59.020
needs.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:04.040
Can we edit Earth?

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:09.299
Back in the day, the land was all one big
lump, a supercontinent called Pangea.

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:13.990
But the shifting plates have really made a
mess of things since then, which is why you

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:18.229
can’t cycle from Melbourne to London anymore.

00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:23.740
One thing we would like is for the ocean current
to once again flow mostly uninterrupted around

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.119
the equator.

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:30.859
We believe this could act to stabilize the
global climate, which, if you haven’t heard,

00:01:30.859 --> 00:01:33.280
is doing about as well as global politics.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:39.399
Right now, the Americas are sort of in the
way when it comes to having connected oceans

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:42.060
so we’re going to have to cut through it.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.469
The idea is to supersize the Panama Canal
to the point where it’s more canal, less

00:01:47.469 --> 00:01:52.399
Panama, basically removing the entire country,
much to the annoyance of Panamanians and hat

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:54.219
lovers across the world.

00:01:54.219 --> 00:01:59.479
It would have to be done by a series of underground
nuclear explosions, or we could just use a

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:01.840
huge pile of Samsung Galaxys.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:10.000
The original canal took about 30 years and
25,000 lives, displacing enough earth to build

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.870
a great wall of china from New York to San
Fransisco, and no one in their right mind

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.440
would want a wall all the way across America.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:25.710
The US actually spent close to a $1 billion
in the 50s and 60s on Project Ploughshare,

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:31.360
looking at the feasibility of using nuclear
weapons as part of large scale engineering

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:37.170
projects such as accessing oil, building artificial
harbours and getting rid of entire countries

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.000
to link two oceans together, apparently.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.430
There’s a fair bit of negotiating to be
done to make this happen and there’s no

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:49.210
scientific consensus on the outcome, with
some arguing that nuking the Isthmus of Panama

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:54.270
would actually hasten the loss of the Greenland
ice sheet, instead of slowing it down, which

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.890
is probably not ideal.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.860
Northern Africa could do with some fresh water
and the nearest river is the Congo.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.920
Unfortunately, getting access involves working
in a region that is still recovering from

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:11.280
a civil war that killed 4 million, so unless
you’re planning on tiptoeing in with a water

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.860
bottle, probably best to look somewhere else.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:20.020
And, if fresh water is what you want, why
not take it from the most abundant source;

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.260
the Amazon.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:27.880
Heinrich Hemmer suggested building a 4,300
kilometer pipe that would pick up fresh water

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.890
from the Amazons river’s mouth.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:38.390
Romanian Physicist Viorel Badescu proposed
a pipe submerged 100m under the surface, anchored

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:39.570
to the sea floor.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:45.810
It would be 30m wide and have 20 pumping stations
to keep the flow going, providing Northern

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.790
Africa with fresh water, as well as a number
of piranhas.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:55.190
Of course, water doesn’t just come from
the ground; it also comes from the sky, roughly

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:59.000
every 18 minutes here in England, on a good
summer day.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.220
But what if you could do with a bit more of
it?

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:07.140
You could try praying to Lono, Hawaiian god
of rain, but since he’s also in charge of

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:11.870
peace, music and sweet potatoes, I doubt you’ll
get an answer.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:16.940
Dubai are taking matters into their own hands
with a plan to build a mountain.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:25.100
The purpose is to force air to rise, thereby
creating more clouds.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:31.150
They already have a number of successful cloud-seeding
projects but they are hoping to find a more

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:36.480
effective solution before all the ground water
is used up washing Lamborghinis with horrifically

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.470
ostentatious paint jobs.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:43.370
The width and height of the mountain are still
being decided and then they can work out how

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:47.900
much it will cost, but it looks like it will
be almost half a trillion dollars.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.240
Although, in other words, the average monthly
salary in Dubai.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.870
Dubai is already pretty experienced at giving
itself plastic surgery though, since it has

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:00.290
been creating man-made islands for over 15
years.

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:07.380
The world’s second largest man-made island
is Yas and it’s due to be completed in 2018.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.120
It will be used to create many schools and
hospit…

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:15.950
no I’m joking, this is Dubai, it’s covered
in Hotels and a Ferrari World theme park.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:20.030
The various island projects started because
the city didn’t have enough beach and they

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:26.270
knew they had to grow tourism to the city
for when the oil eventually runs out.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:31.810
The first major project was Palm Island, which
was designed to fit a long length of beach

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:33.390
in a compact space.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:39.810
First of all, they needed to dredge almost
10 million cubic meters of sand from a nearby

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:44.410
bay and they used this, along with millions
of tonnes of quarried rock, in a range of

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:48.700
sizes including huge 6 ton blocks, to create
the land.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:53.380
They couldn’t have done it without the help
of the world’s greatest land reclaimers;

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.610
the Dutch, who consulted on the project.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:06:05.330
Flevopolder, near Amsterdam, is the largest
man-made island; 970 km2 of land stolen right

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.340
back out the sea.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:15.949
One Dutch Geochemist called Roelof Schuiling
had maybe the grandest idea of all, once again

00:06:15.949 --> 00:06:16.949
in the Middle East.

00:06:16.949 --> 00:06:21.800
At the Strait of Hormuz, where the Gulf Sea
connects with the Indian Ocean, the water

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:24.750
is just 39 kilometers across.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:31.530
Schuiling’s idea was to dam the whole thing,
once the oil trade dies out because right

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:35.730
now there are quite a lot of tankers using
it and a dam would make them very late for

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:36.870
work.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:40.980
Once the dam was in place, the water on one
side would become lower than the other, thanks

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.950
to the process of evaporation.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:49.100
You could then use this difference to generate
an enormous amount of hydro-electric power,

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:56.169
about 2,500 megawatts in this case, which
would power almost two million homes.

00:06:56.169 --> 00:07:01.210
Unfortunately, without the exchange with the
Indian Ocean, the Gulf would get saltier and

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:04.170
saltier so you’d probably kill all the sea
life.

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:09.260
So, dolphins, you’ve got about 20 years
to start producing electricity or you’ve

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:10.389
got it coming.

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:15.350
Maybe start farming electric eels or just
give us back all the oil the BP have so generously

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:21.270
been donating to you over the years, you don’t
even drive; you selfish water mammals.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:40.370
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

